WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.710
Film Courage: Michael, when we first contacted
you to do the interview for the 6-Stage Plot

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:13.299
Structure, we thought it would be fun for
you to break down a script applying your structure.

00:00:13.299 --> 00:00:16.430
You obliged and we were very happy to hear
this.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.090
You chose the movie A FEW GOOD MEN from 1992.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.840
I’m curious why you chose the movie?

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:22.840
Nothing against it, but…

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:23.840
Michael Hauge: Oh, that’s good!

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:24.840
I hope you’re not miserable during this
whole discussion!

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.030
Film Courage: [Laughs] No!

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:27.539
Not at all.

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:31.199
Michael Hauge: A number of reasons.

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:34.570
The first and foremost it’s one of my all
time favorite movies and I don’t really

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.469
enjoy discussing movies that I don’t love.

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:40.629
I don’t really like using bad examples and
so on.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:46.129
I think it is one of the great American screenplays
and movies.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:50.090
Certainly the best courtroom drama since 12
ANGRY MEN.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.750
I have a hard time equivocating between those
two.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.460
Also because it is based on a play written
by Aaron Sorkin.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:03.969
He wrote the script and went on to become
huge as both a screenwriter and a television

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:09.490
writer because this was all pre-dated SPORTS
NIGHT and WEST WING and so on.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.250
So he is now one of the best known screenwriters
in Hollywood, so I thought it would be interesting

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.689
to look at it that way.

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:23.390
But also, the main purpose is to kind of clarify
and make simpler my particular approach to

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.270
story structure and character arc.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:30.390
And because this follows that pattern or that
formula (or whatever you want to call it)

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:37.110
so clearly and in such a straightforward way,
I think it’s a great example because it’s

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:43.520
very easy to see as you pick the movie apart
where all the elements that I talk about in

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.120
structuring character are there.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.500
So I just thought this would be a good, fun
one to talk about.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.500
Film Courage: Okay.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.500
Excellent.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.500
Well let’s get started.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:55.180
So we’ll take the Set Up which is Stage
1 in Act 1.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.190
Do you want to start talking about it?

00:01:58.190 --> 00:01:59.190
Michael Hauge: Sure.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:04.610
Let me give a little backdrop here for those
of you who haven't seen the other interview

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:09.600
we did going into depth of my approach to
the 6 Stage Structure and character arc, I’ll

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.380
review some of that so you’ll be caught
up and understand what I’m talking about.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:20.340
In my approach to structure or plot structure,
I find all movies are always divided into

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.070
6 basic stages.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:28.580
And those stages are created by 5 key turning
points in the script or in the movie.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:35.310
And even more to the point and even neater,
those turning points always occur at essentially

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.360
the same place in every movie.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:44.220
So whatever happens at the 10-percent point
of TITANIC going to to happen at the 10-percent

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.940
point of ZOOTOPIA and is going to happen at
the 10-percent point of A FEW GOOD MEN.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:55.510
So these are things that I think it behooves
screenwriters and filmmakers to model because

00:02:55.510 --> 00:03:00.560
if this has worked consistently in so many
successful movies, you probably want to replicate

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.330
this approach in your own writing.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:09.090
And in terms of character arc, I am going
to get into that and explain that as we go

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.790
through and I start talking about how these
characters are revealed and how we go deeper

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.710
into the characters and we go deeper into
the characters and what their inner journey

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.440
and their arc are and so on like that.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.110
So we’ll get to that part.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.500
As far as the 6 Stages are concerned the first
stage is (as you say) what I call The Set

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.500
Up.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.100
It’s the first 10 percent of the movie or
of the screenplay.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:34.740
As as a screenwriter or filmmaker, there are
a few key things you need to do in that stretch.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.630
First thing you must do is introduce the hero
of the movie.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.390
The hero is just my term for the protagonist.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.390
The main character.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.000
It’s the one we’re routing for.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.800
The character we empathize with or identify
with.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:51.450
It’s the character whose desire defines
the plot of the story and whose desire drives

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.760
it forward.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:58.480
A hero (just to clarify) is not someone who
is heroic.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.300
Unless maybe you’re are talking about a
superhero in a movie.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:04.190
But even then at the beginning not so much
probably.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:11.660
What I call a hero is more a character who
has the potential to become heroic.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:15.720
And in the movie (especially if there’s
a movie with a character arc) the hero has

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.060
to find a certain degree of emotional courage.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.990
That is part of what the story is about, how
do they find their own heroism?

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:26.460
How to they move into the truth of who they
are?

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:32.770
So in the Set-Up of A FEW GOOD MEN, we begin
with something I call an Outside Action Opening.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:38.720
Most of the time in screenplays, the hero
is the first character on the page and when

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.460
you submit your script to be read.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.470
The assumption of the reader will usually
be whoever’s name appears first, that is

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.890
the hero of the story.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:54.620
But you can oftentimes seduce the reader or
the audience into the story more easily if

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:57.620
you begin with the moment of great conflict.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.610
So the movie will begin with something that
does not involve the hero.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:07.190
You’ll see this in THE DA VINCI CODE, it
starts with a serious crime there or a number

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:08.740
of other thrillers and so on.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:16.930
So in A FEW GOOD MEN in opens at the The Bay
base and we see a soldier getting…we’re

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.764
not quite aware of it at the time…but we
see a soldier getting being involved in a

00:05:19.764 --> 00:05:23.840
serious crime and find out he is no longer
alive.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:28.920
Then we cut to another character in the movie.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.560
So we’re still delaying the revelation of
the hero.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:39.110
The effect of that (while it’s a little
dangerous sometimes to have the reader wondering

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:41.400
“Well who am I routing for here?”

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.470
it does create more and more anticipation
“Okay, well who is going to be leading this

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:47.640
parade here?

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:48.700
Where is the hero?”

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.380
So we’re introduced to Lt. Cdr.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.100
JoAnne Galloway, the Demi Moore character.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.640
I’ll talk about more how she functions in
the story when we meet her and find out there

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.040
has been this serious crime.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.430
The navy wants her as an internal affairs
attorney to look into it.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:08.040
They want her to sort of supervise.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.010
She hopes they’ll let her be the attorney.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:11.100
But she is not.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.920
They are going to assign her another attorney.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.950
So all of that is Set-Up and we are wondering
“Well who is going to be assigned to this?”

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:22.880
And since the audience already knows that
it stars Tom Cruise, they’ve probably figured

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:24.350
out it is going to be him.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.350
And then we cut to the hero.

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:29.530
Notice all of this is still in the first 10-percent.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:36.389
So about 5 pages or 5 minutes in, then it
cuts to the Tom Cruise character Lt. Daniel

00:06:36.389 --> 00:06:37.389
Kaffee.

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:41.450
I’m looking down at notes because I am not
always good at remembering character names.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:42.491
But we meet him.

00:06:42.491 --> 00:06:45.240
And what do we meet when we first see him?

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:51.690
He’s out hitting a baseball or a softball
to one of the other sailors or whatever and

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:58.030
another attorney comes up and he’s not shown
up at a meeting (Kaffee hasn’t) and the

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:04.270
guy wants to plea bargain a deal and he wants
to demand 5 years in prison and so on.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:11.360
And we see Kaffee plea bargain that down to
no jail time, just a misdemeanor and so on

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.050
because he says the guy just had a bag of
Oregano.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.620
He didn’t even know.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.520
He just thought is was “Grass” and so
on.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.450
So what does this show us?….And he finally
wins.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.050
So it shows us a few things.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:28.900
First of all, because we feel sort of sorry
for the guy who had the bag of Oregano, we

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:33.330
start to like Kaffee immediately because he’s
on the side of this other guy.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:34.360
He’s a likable guy.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:38.750
And he seems well-liked by the guy he’s
playing catch with or hitting to ball to and

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:39.930
so on.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:45.889
So being likable is one key way to establish
empathy with a hero.

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:49.670
That is one of the main things you have to
do as soon as you introduce the hero.

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:52.230
The other thing we find out is he’s highly-skilled.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:54.160
So we admire the character.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.570
He may be kind of slick and quick about how
he does it but it’s kind of amazing how

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:05.550
he was able to get 5 years in the Brig down
to no jail time and a misdemeanor and just

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:09.660
‘something on his record that’s going
to be meaningless.’

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.710
In other words, he gets the guy off.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:15.970
So we’re very admiring of that about him.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:21.240
And the way he does it is very funny and we’re
drawn to characters that make us laugh oftentimes

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:24.670
they also have the courage to say things we
wouldn’t have the courage to say.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:29.200
They’re not real politically correct and
he’s very sarcastic and puts people down

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:30.260
and so on.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:36.590
So all of this is to introduce us to this
character and create empathy with this hero.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:39.492
And then we come to the 10-percent point of
the movie….(Stayed tuned for the continuation

00:08:39.492 --> 00:08:40.492
via the second video from Michael Hauge).

00:08:40.492 --> 00:08:40.508
Question for the Viewers: What movie do you
consider to be the best court room drama?

